::ENG4_U05_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The board approved an interim plan until the permanent policy was complete. What does interim mean? {
~A. final.#Incorrect. The permanent policy is not complete yet.
=B. temporary.#Correct. Until the permanent policy shows interim means temporary.
~C. unrelated.#Incorrect. The plan is related to the policy period.
~D. illegal.#Incorrect. Legality is not suggested.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The word reversible contains reverse and -ible. What does reversible mean? {
~A. impossible to change.#Incorrect. That is the opposite.
~B. moving forward quickly.#Incorrect. The word parts do not support this.
~C. spoken repeatedly.#Incorrect. The word parts do not refer to speech.
=D. able to be changed back.#Correct. Reverse means turn back, and -ible means capable of.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The policy had a narrow scope, applying only to public buildings. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. The policy applied only to public buildings.#Correct. This preserves the limited scope.
~B. The policy applied everywhere.#Incorrect. Narrow scope means limited range.
~C. The policy was impossible to understand.#Incorrect. Scope is range, not clarity.
~D. The policy was cancelled.#Incorrect. The passage does not say it was cancelled.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The testimony was corroborated by video evidence. Which context clue helps define corroborated? {
~A. testimony.#Incorrect. Testimony names the statement, not the confirming clue.
~B. was.#Incorrect. Was does not define the term.
=C. by video evidence.#Correct. Video evidence supports or confirms the testimony.
~D. evidence was ignored.#Incorrect. The passage says evidence supported the testimony.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Reference entry: acute means sharp; severe; highly perceptive. Passage: The nurse noticed acute pain after the injury. Which meaning fits? {
~A. highly perceptive.#Incorrect. Pain is not perceptive.
~B. mathematically angled.#Incorrect. That meaning is not relevant to pain.
=C. severe or sharp.#Correct. Injury and pain support the severe or sharp meaning.
~D. temporary only.#Incorrect. Acute does not necessarily mean temporary here.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The phrase status quo means: {
=A. the existing state of things.#Correct. Status quo refers to the current condition.
~B. a sudden emergency.#Incorrect. Status quo is not an emergency.
~C. a legal punishment.#Incorrect. It does not mean punishment.
~D. a scientific control group.#Incorrect. That is not the phrase meaning.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The author's claim was qualified by several exceptions. What is the best paraphrase? {
~A. The author made a claim with no limits.#Incorrect. Qualified means limited.
~B. The author refused to make a claim.#Incorrect. A claim was made.
~C. The author's claim was unrelated to the topic.#Incorrect. The passage does not imply unrelatedness.
=D. The author limited the claim by adding exceptions.#Correct. This preserves qualified and exceptions.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The article contrasts a comprehensive review with a brief summary. What does comprehensive mean? {
~A. short and casual.#Incorrect. It contrasts with brief.
=B. complete or broad in scope.#Correct. Comprehensive contrasts with brief summary.
~C. unsupported and weak.#Incorrect. Support is not the issue.
~D. confusing and vague.#Incorrect. The context concerns scope.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The root cred means believe. Which word most clearly means believable? {
~A. creditless.#Incorrect. That is not the standard meaning for believable.
~B. credentialed.#Incorrect. Credentialed means having qualifications.
=C. credible.#Correct. Credible means able to be believed.
~D. incredulous.#Incorrect. Incredulous means unwilling to believe.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The director requested a concise memo, not a lengthy report. What does concise mean? {
=A. brief and clear.#Correct. Concise means expressed briefly and clearly.
~B. long and detailed.#Incorrect. The contrast rejects lengthy.
~C. emotional and personal.#Incorrect. The context does not suggest emotion.
~D. unfinished.#Incorrect. Concise does not mean incomplete.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The decision was unanimous; every board member supported it. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Some board members opposed the decision.#Incorrect. Every member supported it.
~B. The board delayed the decision.#Incorrect. Delay is not mentioned.
~C. The decision was informal.#Incorrect. Formality is not addressed.
=D. All board members agreed with the decision.#Correct. This preserves unanimous.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Reference entry: sanction means official permission; a penalty. Passage: The agency gave its sanction before construction began. Which meaning fits? {
~A. a penalty.#Incorrect. Construction began after agency sanction, so permission fits.
=B. official permission.#Correct. The context shows approval before construction.
~C. a disagreement.#Incorrect. The agency did not disagree.
~D. a delay.#Incorrect. The sentence does not indicate delay.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The prefix sub- often means under. What does substandard most likely mean? {
~A. above the required level.#Incorrect. Sub- suggests below.
=B. below an acceptable standard.#Correct. Substandard means below standard.
~C. equal in quality.#Incorrect. The prefix indicates below.
~D. unrelated to standards.#Incorrect. Standard is part of the word.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The scientist isolated the variable to test its effect. What does isolated mean in this context? {
~A. made lonely.#Incorrect. The subject is an experiment, not emotion.
~B. destroyed permanently.#Incorrect. Testing does not imply destruction.
=C. separated for study.#Correct. Scientific context supports separated for testing.
~D. praised publicly.#Incorrect. Praise is not implied.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The contract contains a clause that permits renewal after one year. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. One part of the contract allows renewal after a year.#Correct. Clause means a section, and permits means allows.
~B. The contract forbids renewal.#Incorrect. Permits means allows.
~C. The contract requires immediate cancellation.#Incorrect. Cancellation is not mentioned.
~D. The contract has no time limit.#Incorrect. One year is stated.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The data were inconsistent, with results changing from trial to trial. What does inconsistent mean? {
~A. perfectly repeated.#Incorrect. That is the opposite.
~B. unrelated to trials.#Incorrect. Trials define the context.
~C. hidden from view.#Incorrect. Visibility is not addressed.
=D. not staying the same.#Correct. Changing from trial to trial defines inconsistent.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The suffix -less means without. What does consequence-less most nearly mean? {
~A. full of results.#Incorrect. -less means without.
~B. before consequences.#Incorrect. The suffix does not mean before.
=C. without consequences.#Correct. The suffix shows absence.
~D. legally required.#Incorrect. The word parts do not support this.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The committee deferred the vote until more financial data were available. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. The committee postponed the vote until it had more financial data.#Correct. This preserves deferred and until.
~B. The committee held the vote immediately.#Incorrect. Deferred means postponed.
~C. The committee cancelled the project permanently.#Incorrect. Postponed does not mean cancelled.
~D. The committee ignored financial data.#Incorrect. It waited for more data.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Reference entry: novel means new or original; a long fictional work. Passage: The engineer proposed a novel solution to the design problem. Which meaning fits? {
~A. a long fictional work.#Incorrect. The context is engineering.
=B. new or original.#Correct. A novel solution is new or original.
~C. a printed book.#Incorrect. The sentence concerns a solution.
~D. impossible to build.#Incorrect. Novel does not mean impossible.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The root port means carry. Which word most directly uses that meaning? {
~A. portrait.#Incorrect. Portrait is not about carrying.
~B. portion.#Incorrect. Portion means a part.
~C. portal.#Incorrect. Portal means doorway.
=D. portable.#Correct. Portable means able to be carried.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The recommendations were tentative because the study was still incomplete. What does tentative mean? {
=A. not final or still uncertain.#Correct. Because the study is incomplete, recommendations are not final.
~B. aggressive and hostile.#Incorrect. The context does not suggest hostility.
~C. fully proven.#Incorrect. Incomplete study rejects certainty.
~D. unrelated to evidence.#Incorrect. The study provides evidence context.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The policy applies exclusively to emergency requests. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. The policy applies to all requests.#Incorrect. Exclusively means only.
~B. The policy never applies.#Incorrect. It applies to a specific category.
=C. The policy applies only to emergency requests.#Correct. This preserves exclusively.
~D. The policy applies after emergencies end.#Incorrect. Timing is not stated.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.V.02] The phrase de facto means: {
~A. by law only.#Incorrect. That is closer to de jure.
~B. for this specific purpose.#Incorrect. That is closer to ad hoc.
~C. before the fact.#Incorrect. That is not the phrase meaning.
=D. in fact or in practice.#Correct. De facto means existing in practice.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: The consultant's role was peripheral; she advised the team but did not make final decisions. What does peripheral mean? {
~A. central and controlling.#Incorrect. She did not make final decisions.
=B. minor or not central.#Correct. Advising without final authority shows a peripheral role.
~C. physical and medical.#Incorrect. The context is role, not body.
~D. permanent and legal.#Incorrect. The sentence does not discuss permanence or legality.
}

::ENG4_U05_L04_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The article distinguishes correlation from causation, warning that two events occurring together does not prove one caused the other. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. The article explains that events happening together do not automatically show cause and effect.#Correct. This accurately preserves the distinction.
~B. The article says correlation always proves causation.#Incorrect. The passage warns against that.
~C. The article says events never relate to each other.#Incorrect. It does not deny all relationships.
~D. The article focuses only on grammar.#Incorrect. The topic is evidence and cause.
}
